  "public": true,
  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:hazop",
  "domain": "governance",
  "definition": "HAZOP (Hazard and Operability Study) is a structured, systematic technique for identifying potential hazards and operability problems in a process or system by applying a set of guide words \u2014 such as 'more', 'less', 'no', and 'reverse' \u2014 to each design intention and examining the consequences of deviation. It is widely used in process industries and safety-critical engineering, and is referenced by functional-safety standards such as IEC 61508. A HAZOP study is typically conducted by a multidisciplinary team working systematically through a piping and instrumentation diagram or equivalent process representation.",
